Stop Open MPI from overriding the CPU pinning.

**Patches 0008 and 0010 never reached the MPI ranks.** Both work by
`taskset`ing this script so its children inherit the mask. Every plain
`fork`/`exec` child does. `mpirun` does too -- and then discards it for the
ranks it spawns.

Verified on the station on 2026-08-13, minutes after 0010 was applied, with
`taskset -cp 2-7 $$` already in force at the top of this script:

    9833  rx_uhd_ext_gps      0,1     <- correct, 0010 worked
    9883  mpirun (detect)     2-7     <- correct, inherited
    9906  detect rank 0       0,1     <- the recorder's core
    9907  detect rank 1       2,3
    9913  mpirun (calc)       2-7     <- correct, inherited
    9925  calc  rank 0        0,1     <- the recorder's core
    9926  calc  rank 1        2,3

Open MPI calls `sched_setaffinity` on each rank after fork. Version 1.10.2
(what Ubuntu 16.04 ships, confirmed by `mpirun --version`) defaults to
`--bind-to core` for `-np <= 2`, and assigns ranks straight off the machine
topology: rank 0 to physical core 0, which on this four-core part is CPUs 0
*and* 1. The mask it inherited is not consulted.

So on a box whose entire tuning story is "fence the recorder off", **two
compute ranks were placed on the recorder's core**, while cores 4-7 -- a third
of the machine -- sat unused.

**This also corrects the diagnosis in patch 0010.** 0010 says the residual
3.4% came from a consumer on `cpu1`, the recorder's sibling. It was worse than
that: under 0008's `taskset -cp 1-7`, rank 0 was still placed on `0,1`, so a
rank was running on `cpu0` itself -- the one CPU the mask excluded by name. The
recorder did not have a dedicated hyperthread either. 0010 is still right and
still required; only its account of the mechanism was too generous.

It explains 0008's numbers, too. 0008 measured a real 96% drop reduction, and
if the ranks were never fenced that has to have come from somewhere else: the
other nine processes -- `station_monitor.py`, `drf ringbuffer`, the supervising
shells, the receivers still running then -- which are ordinary children and did
inherit the mask. Moving those off was the win. The two ranks are the part that
never moved, and the part still costing 3.4% every morning since.

**Why `--bind-to none` and not `--cpu-set`.** `--cpu-set 2-7 --bind-to core`
would keep core-level cache locality. But 1.10's `--cpu-set` takes *logical*
processor ids, and misreading it lands a rank back on core 0 -- the exact
failure this patch ends. Inheriting the script's mask is worth more here than
the locality; the kernel will spread the ranks across 2-7 on its own.

**One line, at the variable rather than the two call sites.** `$MPIRUN` is
unquoted at both (lines 138 and 195), so word-splitting expands this to
`mpirun --bind-to none -np ...` in each.

**Applying this does not require a restart to be worth doing, but it does
require one to take effect.** Open MPI binds at rank startup and never
re-asserts it, so `taskset -pc 2-7 <rank pids>` fixes a running station
immediately; that hand-fix lapses the next time the supervising loop restarts
either `mpirun`, which is what this patch is for.

**Verify on the ranks, not on the launcher.** `mpirun` reading `2-7` is
precisely the reading that hid this for two days:

    for p in $(pgrep -f detect_chirps.py) $(pgrep -f calc_ionograms.py); do
        taskset -pc $p
    done

All of them must read `2-7`. Only `rx_uhd_ext_gps` may read `0,1`.

diff --git a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
--- a/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
+++ b/examples/marieluise/dombas.sh
@@ -33,7 +33,18 @@ taskset -cp 2-7 $$ > /dev/null
 
 # Make sure this is the right mpirun command
 # (you might need mpirun instead of mpirun.mpich)
-MPIRUN=mpirun
+#
+# --bind-to none is load-bearing, not tuning. Open MPI calls sched_setaffinity
+# on every rank after fork, and 1.10.2 -- what this box runs -- defaults to
+# --bind-to core for -np <= 2. It then places rank 0 on physical core 0 from
+# the machine's own topology, with no reference to the mask it inherited.
+# Measured here on 2026-08-13, with the taskset above already in place: both
+# mpirun processes read 2-7, while a detect rank and a calc rank read 0,1 --
+# the recorder's core -- and cores 4-7 sat unused. Without this flag the
+# taskset ten lines up does nothing whatsoever for the ranks that do the work.
+#
+# If you switch to MPICH, drop the flag: Hydra does not bind by default.
+MPIRUN="mpirun --bind-to none"
 
 # ram disk buffer for fast i/o.
 # if you have a fast SSD or raid, you can also use that
